  "text": " To find the coordinates of the center of the circumscribed sphere as a function of the coordinates $(x,y,z)$ of point $P$, we can use the fact that the radius of the sphere is half the length of the cube's edge, which is 1. The center of the sphere is equidistant from all vertices of the cube.\n\nLet's find the coordinates of the center of the sphere:\n1. The center of the cube is at the origin $(0,0,0)$.\n2. Since the radius of the sphere is 1, the center of the sphere will be at the same distance from the edge of the cube. Let's denote the center of the sphere as $(a,b,c)$. Since the sphere is tangent to the axes at the points where they intersect the cube, the distance from $(a,b,c)$ to any axis must be 1.\n\nConsider the $y$-axis (which is the line $z=0$):\nThe distance from $(a,b,c)$ to the $y$-axis is $|c|$. Since the sphere is tangent to the $y$-axis, $|c|=1$.\n\nSimilarly, consider the $z$-axis (which is the line $x=0$):\nThe distance from $(a,b,c)$ to the $z$-axis is $|a|$. Since the sphere is tangent to the $z$-axis, $|a|=1$.\n\nAnd, consider the $x$-axis (which is the line $y=0$):\nThe distance from $(a,b,c)$ to the $x$-axis is $|b|$. Since the sphere is tangent to the $x$-axis, $|b|=1$.\n\nThus, the center of the sphere $(a,b,c)$ must satisfy $a = \\pm 1$, $b = \\pm 1$, and $c = 1$ (considering $|c| = 1$ and that the sphere is tangential to the $y$-axis).\n\nFor simplicity, let's take $(a,b,c) = (1,1,1)$. Thus, the center of the sphere is $(1,1,1)$.\n\nFinally, verifying the tangency:\nThe distance from the center $(1,1,1)$ to any vertex of the cube, e.g., $(1,1,1)$, is indeed 1, which verifies the tangency of the sphere to the cube's vertices.\n\nStep-by-step reasoning and proof:\n1. Calculate the radius of the sphere for a cube with edge 2.\n2. Use the fact that the sphere is tangential to the coordinate axes at their intersection with the cube.\n3. Find the center of the sphere by equating the distance from the center to the axes.\n4. Verify the tangency.\n\n\\boxed{(1,1,1)}",
